  The cart says this itself. Its romdev.recompile custom section (for a
  sync32 .s32, the metadata in rd_meta.c) has a `licence` entry:
    spdx          the SPDX expression above, e.g. "GPL-2.0-or-later"
    terms         one plain line per licence
    upstream      [{name, url, commit, spdx}] of each transplanted component
    source        the romdevtools version and directory holding the ported
                  code: that package, at that version, is where the
                  corresponding source of the emulator code is
    licenceTexts  where the full licence texts are (romdevtools LICENSES/)
    romCode       the reminder about the game's own code (below)
    notices       verbatim, the notices that must travel with every copy
                  (the non-commercial licences, Musashi, snes9x, and the
                  MIT / ISC notices); the standard GPL, LGPL and MPL texts
                  are named by SPDX id and found in LICENSES/
  The entry depends only on the platform, the cart's chips and the romdev
  version, so the same inputs still build a byte-identical cart.

  The game inside. A cart recompiled from someone else's game also contains
  that game's code (and any BIOS, firmware, MSU-1 data or add-on images built
  into it). It can only be shared with the game owner's permission, whatever
  the emulator licences allow.

  blip_buf and snes_spc are LGPL-2.1 libraries inside non-commercial cores;
  the carts combine them the same way the upstream cores do. This section
  describes what each licence says; it is not legal advice. If you plan to
  distribute recompiled carts, read the licences in LICENSES/.

C64 ROMs - free replacements, not Commodore's
----------------------------------------------
  Upstream vice-libretro compiles Commodore's KERNAL, BASIC and CHARGEN ROMs
  into the core (USE_EMBEDDED). Those images are "Copyright C by Commodore
  Business Machines" per VICE's own README; the GPL covers VICE's code, not
  them. romdev builds VICE with that define disabled and redistributes none of
  them.

  In their place romdev-core-vice bundles MEGA65 Open ROMs (GPL-3.0 /
  LGPL-3.0), a clean-room KERNAL/BASIC/CHARGEN, so the C64 works out of the
  box. Licences ship alongside the images in that package's roms/ directory.
  Set ROMDEV_C64_ROM_DIR to use original ROMs you own instead.

SNES boot ROM and chip data - romdev's own, or the user's
--------------------------------------------------------
  The SPC700 sound CPU starts from a 64-byte boot ROM (the IPL) that runs the
  upload protocol over ports $2140-$2143. Upstream snes9x and bsnes embed
  Nintendo's. romdev uses its own instead: a clean-room reimplementation
  written from the public description of the protocol and of the state it
  hands over (romdevtools scripts/spc700-ipl/ipl.asm, MIT), assembled with
  asar. The snes9x core, the bsnes core and every recompiled SNES cart run
  it; the builds replace the upstream array (scripts/spc700-ipl/install-ipl.mjs).

  romdev ships no chip firmware, chip data ROM or boot ROM. snes9x embeds the
  DSP-1 data ROM (and a sine table taken from it), the DSP-3 data ROM and the
  ST010's data ROM tables; bsnes embeds the DSP-1 data ROM, the ST010 tables,
  the Cx4 data ROM and the Super Game Boy boot ROMs. romdev builds both with
  those tables emptied (scripts/recompile-gen/snes9x-chips/hle-data.mjs,
  scripts/patches/bsnes/strip-firmware.mjs) and takes the user's files
  instead: snes9x reads dsp1b.data.rom, dsp3.data.rom or st010.data.rom from
  its system directory, a recompiled cart is built with the same file
  (options.firmware), and bsnes reads each chip's firmware (and sgb_boot.bin /
  sgb2_boot.bin) from its system directory. Without the file such a cart is
  refused with the file and its size named; bsnes runs a Cx4 cart with its
  high-level emulation when cx4.data.rom is absent.

